body:
I downloaded the code and was able to add AutoMapper.WPA81 to my solution and debug it directly.  The issue appears to be related to the fact that AutoMapper.TypeInfo.GetAllPublicMembers gets public members for all types up the inheritance chain (adding BaseTypes to typesToScan).  That seems reasonable.
But then the code in AutoMapper.ConfigurationStore::CreateMappingExpression that handles IgnoreMapAttribute gets hit.  That code calls mappingExp.ForMember, which ultimately calls TypeExtensions.GetProperty on my destination type (UserInfo in this case).  Here's that function:

```
    public static PropertyInfo GetProperty(this Type type, string name)
    {
        return type.GetTypeInfo().GetDeclaredProperty(name);
    }
```

GetDeclaredProperty will only return properties declared on the actual type, not properties declared on base types.  So the ModelBase.Errors property is found in GetAllPublicMembers, but it isn't found when the code tries to ignore it.
Not sure exactly what to do about that...but I'll probably add a TypeExtensions.GetPropertyDeep method for now that will walk up the BaseType looking for the property being requested, and then modify MappingExpression.ForMember to use that instead.  That seems the least likely to cause unexpected side effects for me.

Super quick and dirty, but it got me back up and running:

```
        public static PropertyInfo GetPropertyDeep(this Type type, string name) {
            PropertyInfo pi = null;
            var walker = type;
            while (pi == null && walker != null) {
                pi = walker.GetTypeInfo().GetDeclaredProperty(name);
                walker = walker.BaseType();
            }
            return pi;
        }
```

And in MappingExpression:

```
        public IMappingExpression<TSource, TDestination> ForMember(
            string name,
            Action<IMemberConfigurationExpression<TSource>> memberOptions)
        {
            IMemberAccessor destMember = null;
            var propertyInfo = TypeMap.DestinationType.GetPropertyDeep(name);
```

issue.body:
### Source/destination types

```csharp
public class Source<T> : List<T>
{
  public String PropertyToMap { get; set; }
  public String PropertyToIgnore { get; set; } = "I am not ignored";
}

public class Destination : DestinationBase<String>
{
}

public abstract class DestinationBase<T> : DestinationBaseBase, IDestinationBase<T>
{
  private String m_PropertyToIgnore;

  public virtual String PropertyToMap { get; set; }

  [IgnoreMap]
  public override String PropertyToIgnore
  {
    get
    {
      return m_PropertyToIgnore ?? (m_PropertyToIgnore = "Ignore me");
    }
    set
    {
      m_PropertyToIgnore = value;
    }
  }

  public virtual List<T> Items { get; set; }
}

public abstract class DestinationBaseBase
{
  [IgnoreMap]
  public virtual String PropertyToIgnore { get; set; }
}

public interface IDestinationBase<T>
{
  String PropertyToMap { get; set; }
  List<T> Items { get; set; }
}
```

### Mapping configuration

```csharp
Mapper.Initialize(config => config.AddProfile<AutomapperProfile>());

public AutomapperProfile()
{
  CreateMap(typeof (IList<>), typeof (IDestinationBase<>))
    .ForMember(nameof(IDestinationBase<Object>.Items), p_Expression => p_Expression.MapFrom(p_Source => p_Source));
}
```

### Version: 6.1.0
<!-- Include the major/minor version (4.2.1, 5.2.0, 6.0.2 etc) --> 

### Expected behavior

The source object mapped to the destination object, except the property 'PropertyToIgnore' and no exceptions or test failures.

### Actual behavior

ArgumentOutOfRangeException on Mapper.Map.
Has similarities with issue https://github.com/AutoMapper/AutoMapper/issues/844.

### Steps to reproduce

```csharp
var l_Source = new Source<String>
{
  "Cat",
  "Dog",
};
l_Source.PropertyToMap = "Hello World";
var l_Destination = Mapper.Map<Destination>(l_Source);

Debug.Assert(l_Destination.PropertyToIgnore == "Ignore me", "Destination property 'PropertyToIgnore' is invalid.");
```

